# rules: swift-sensitive-value-logged
# languages: swift
# fixture: swift/CWE-532.swift
# from: CWE-532 (Insertion of Sensitive Information into Log File), MASVS-STORAGE-2
#
# `print` and `NSLog` go to the unified log, and the unified log is not a
# developer-only place. It is gathered into the sysdiagnose bundle support asks
# the user to send, it is readable in Console.app by anyone the phone is paired
# with, and on a shared machine the simulator's copy is readable by every
# account. A secret written there has a lifetime the app does not control and
# no way to revoke.
#
# The whole difficulty is telling a secret from a message about one.
# `print("password rejected")` is a sentence with the word in it and is not a
# finding; `print(password)` is the password. What separates them is that the
# second carries a *value* - a bare name, or a `\(...)` interpolation - and the
# first is only letters.
#
# A template that interpolates something innocent beside the word is kept on
# purpose: `"password for \(user)"` is reported, because working out which half
# of the template the word belongs to would be guessing, and this is the
# direction that costs a false positive rather than a leaked credential.
#
# Swift has no non-interpolating string literal, so unlike PHP there is no
# constant-versus-interpolated question to ask: a `\(` in a Swift string is
# always code.

[ "print($MSG)", "print($MSG, $$$_)",
  "NSLog($MSG)", "NSLog($MSG, $$$_)",
  "debugPrint($MSG)", "debugPrint($MSG, $$$_)",
  "dump($MSG)", "dump($MSG, $$$_)" ] as $logs
| "(?i)passw|secret|token|api[_-]?key|credential|private[_-]?key|session[_-]?id"
  as $namesASecret
| "\\\\\\(" as $interpolates
| "^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z_0-9.]*$" as $isABareName
| scan_ast("*.swift"; $logs) as $all
| ( ($all | of($logs) | where_capture("MSG"; $namesASecret)
     | where_capture("MSG"; $interpolates))
    + ($all | of($logs) | where_capture("MSG"; $namesASecret)
       | where_capture("MSG"; $isABareName)) )
| focus("MSG")
| finding("swift-sensitive-value-logged";
    "$MSG puts a secret in the unified log, which leaves the phone in every "
    + "sysdiagnose and is readable by anyone the device is paired with")
| report
